<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
FORGE team-configurable OWASP Dependency-Check suppression file.

Suppresses findings from esbuild/vite/vitest and their transitive
dependencies: confirmed dev-only npm tooling (devDependencies), never
present in any deployed frontend Docker image, so a vulnerability in this
subtree carries a categorically different (near-zero) real-world risk here
than a production dependency finding. This is a package-identity-based
suppression (any finding under these dependency trees), not a per-CVE
allowlist; matches this file's own justification, and mirrors
team/gitleaks-allowlist.toml's approach of excluding a whole category of
path, not chasing individual false positives one at a time.

Confirmed live against REQ-2026-03's frontend dependency-check report
(2026-08-19): dependency-check's Node/npm analyzer reports each
lockfile-derived dependency's filePath as ".../package-lock.json?ROOT",
where ROOT is the top-level package that pulled it in, e.g.
"launch-editor" (a vite transitive dependency) is reported under
"package-lock.json?vite", not under its own package name. filePath
matching on that "?ROOT" suffix is therefore the correct mechanism here,
not packageUrl (which would only match the top-level package's own name,
missing its transitive dependencies).

Do NOT add next/react findings here: those are production dependencies
(shipped in the deployed image) and are a separate, already-tracked,
already-accepted risk (see CLAUDE.md Open Items: "8 HIGH-severity
next@14.2.35 CVE findings have no 14.x backport"). Suppressing them would
hide a real, in-production risk category, not a dev-tooling one.

Each <suppress> block also carries <vulnerabilityName regex="true">.*</vulnerabilityName>
purely to satisfy the XSD, which requires at least one vulnerability-matching
child element (cve/vulnerabilityName/cwe/cvssBelow/etc.) after the file/package
matcher: a file/package matcher alone is REJECTED (confirmed live: the CI
run this file was first added in silently failed to load with a
SuppressionParseException, meaning dependency-check ran with no suppression
applied at all until this fix). The actual suppression logic here is the
filePath match; the vulnerabilityName wildcard is a required formality, not
an additional filter.

Schema: https://dependency-check.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/suppression.html
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.4.xsd">
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            esbuild is a devDependency (bundler used by Vite/Vitest during
            local dev/test only) -- never present in the deployed frontend
            Docker image.
        ]]></notes>
        <filePath regex="true">.*package-lock\.json\?esbuild$</filePath>
        <vulnerabilityName regex="true">.*</vulnerabilityName>
    </suppress>
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            vite is a devDependency (used only by Vitest's test runner) --
            never present in the deployed frontend Docker image. Covers
            vite's own findings and transitive dev-tooling dependencies
            dependency-check attributes to this root (e.g. launch-editor).
        ]]></notes>
        <filePath regex="true">.*package-lock\.json\?vite$</filePath>
        <vulnerabilityName regex="true">.*</vulnerabilityName>
    </suppress>
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            vitest is a devDependency (test runner, including its optional
            UI/browser-mode server) -- never present in the deployed
            frontend Docker image, so its UI dev-server findings (e.g.
            GHSA-5xrq-8626-4rwp, a Windows-only arbitrary file read when
            the Vitest UI server is exposed to a network) do not apply to
            this application's real deployed attack surface.
        ]]></notes>
        <filePath regex="true">.*package-lock\.json\?vitest$</filePath>
        <vulnerabilityName regex="true">.*</vulnerabilityName>
    </suppress>
</suppressions>
